// Test: bugfixes/json_get_array_oob_returns_empty_string
// Semantic: stdlib-reference.md §8 — json.get returns an empty string
//   (not null) when the path does not resolve, so the canonical iteration
//   shape `while item != "": item = json.get(arr, i.toString())` can
//   terminate portably across all hosts.
// Regression: WASM-TRAP-JSON-ARRAY-ITER fingerprint 156e745b63d9
//   (first seen 0.33.85). Iterating a top-level JSON array with the
//   canonical `while item != ""` termination shape trapped or looped
//   forever on clean-server and clean-node-server, while the exact same
//   compiled WASM ran to completion on the standalone wasmtime_runner.
//
// Root cause: `json.get` (src/stdlib/json_class.rs :244-352) returned
//   raw pointer 0 (null) when the path didn't resolve — both the outer
//   null-guard and the `__json_get_path` miss path. Callers store the
//   result into a `string` variable and compare with `""`. The host's
//   `string_compare` bridge reads the string length from `mem[ptr]` with
//   no null guard. On the standalone runner memory[0..4] is zero, so
//   null reads as an empty string and the comparison happens to work.
//   On real hosts memory near address 0 contains data-segment bytes
//   (WASM data initializers), so length reads as garbage — either a
//   huge unsigned value that walks off memory and traps, or a random
//   non-zero value that makes `item != ""` always evaluate true, causing
//   an infinite loop that eventually OOMs / traps on stack exhaustion.
//
// Fix: json.get now wraps its return so any 0/null result is replaced
//   with a pointer to a valid Clean Language empty string (4 bytes of
//   `[0,0,0,0]` on the bump heap). The compiler owns the WASM contract
//   with hosts: string-returning bridge exports must never return null.
//   The alternative — patching each host's string_compare to null-guard —
//   would leave the same latent hazard in string_concat, string_length,
//   substring, and every other host string function.
//
// Fixed in: compiler 0.33.99
//
// Expected output:
//   {"title":"A"}
//   {"title":"B"}
//   {"title":"C"}
//   done

start:
	string arr = "[{\"title\":\"A\"},{\"title\":\"B\"},{\"title\":\"C\"}]"
	integer i = 0
	string item = json.get(arr, "0")
	while item != ""
		print(item)
		i = i + 1
		item = json.get(arr, i.toString())
	print("done")
